            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Where Should I Put the Budget First When Starting an Independent Website?</h2>
<p>Start with Meta, then expand to Google. During the cold start phase of a new account, Meta's creative testing costs are lower and scaling is faster, allowing you to verify product-audience fit in a short time.</p>
<p>Specific ratio reference: Put 70% of the budget to Meta in the early stage and 30% to Google Ads. Once Meta delivers 3-5 stable conversion creatives, gradually increase the Google budget to 40%-50%.</p>
<p>This judgment is based on two facts: Meta's audience targeting is more precise, suitable for new product testing; Google search ads are more suitable for products that already have some brand search volume.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Much Daily Budget Should I Set to Avoid Waste?</h2>
<p>The daily budget for a single channel should not be less than $20. Below this threshold, the algorithm's learning data is insufficient, and the account will repeatedly consume budget in the "exploration phase" without finding the conversion audience.</p>
<p>If your monthly total budget is only $3,000, focus on one channel rather than dispersing $1,500 across Meta and Google. Dispersed delivery will cause both channels to fail to learn effectively.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When budget is limited, it's better to do precise targeted small-budget testing than to waste broad delivery.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>How Do I Determine the Budget Ratio Between Google Ads and Meta Ads?</h2>
<p>There's no universal ratio, but there's judgment logic:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard products, essential items</strong> (like tools, home supplies): Google search ads can account for 50%-60%, as users have strong active search intent.</li>
<li><strong>Non-standard products, impulse consumer goods</strong> (like fashion accessories, creative gifts): Meta feed ads should account for 60%-70%, relying on interest targeting and creative stimulation.</li>
<li><strong>Niche categories or blue ocean products</strong>: First use Meta for audience education, then increase Google investment once search volume picks up.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>How Do I Allocate Budget When Running Multiple Campaigns Simultaneously?</h2>
<p>Prioritize ensuring the main campaigns have sufficient budget to "eat their fill." Put 80% of the budget into 1-2 best-performing campaigns, and distribute the remaining 20% across 2-3 new campaigns for testing.</p>
<p>Don't distribute equally. Running $50 daily budget across 5 campaigns may seem diversified, but in reality, each campaign is in a "not full" state, and the algorithm cannot learn effectively.</p>
<p>The only standard for budget allocation priority is: <strong>Who is stably generating sales should get more money.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Should I Increase the Budget During End-of-Season Promotions?</h2>
<p>You can increase it, but control the pace. Start gradually increasing the budget 2 weeks before the promotion, with each increase not exceeding 20%, giving the algorithm sufficient adaptation time.</p>
<p>Don't suddenly double the budget on promotion day—the traffic will surge, but conversion rates are often lower than usual because users are overwhelmed by promotional information and need longer decision cycles. Your money may be spent, but ROI will be难看.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The prerequisite for increasing budget is that the creative and landing page have been verified; otherwise, increasing money equals accelerating the burning of trial costs.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>How Do I Determine If Poor Results Are Due to Budget Issues or Creative Issues?</h2>
<p>A simple judgment method:</p>
<ul>
<li>If CPA (Cost Per Acquisition) is high but conversion volume is stable, it means creative appeal is insufficient and needs optimization.</li>
<li>If CPA is high and conversion volume is also very low, it indicates a budget allocation or targeting problem—possibly the budget is too low for the algorithm to run, or the audience circle is too narrow.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the latter, try doubling the budget for 3 days and observe. If CPA decreases significantly, it confirms a budget shortage; if CPA doesn't change, the problem is with the creative or product itself.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>In summary</strong>: The core of budget allocation is "concentrate first, then disperse." For new accounts, concentrate resources to run through one channel first; once the model stabilizes, expand to multi-channel distribution. For multiple campaigns, ensure the main campaigns eat their fill and keep testing campaigns at a small budget. Dynamic adjustment is more important than setting a fixed budget once; review delivery data every two weeks and reallocate ratios based on CPA changes.</p>
